Don't let one bad bookmark tag stop gossip from starting.

gossip_lib::init() loads the stored kind-10003 bookmark list before anything
else runs, and BookmarkList::add_tags() propagated any tag it could not parse.
A single malformed tag arriving from a relay therefore became a poison pill:
every later startup aborted with

    Error: Error { kind: Nostr(TagMismatch), file: None, line: None }

before the UI -- or any subcommand, since init() precedes argument handling --
ever ran, so nothing shipped with gossip could repair the database.

The tag that triggered it here was ["title", ""]. nostr-types' get_opt_index()
treats an empty string as absent, so the "title" arm of Tag::parse() hits
`.ok_or(Error::TagMismatch)?`. add_tags() would have discarded that tag anyway:
it only builds bookmarks out of Event and Address tags and sends everything
else to `_ => continue`. So the tag was never usable, it only killed startup.

Skipping a tag that fails to parse is therefore consistent with how the rest of
the function treats tags it can't use. Log it at warn so the offending tag is
identifiable rather than silently dropped.

--- a/gossip-lib/src/bookmarks.rs
+++ b/gossip-lib/src/bookmarks.rs
@@ -19,7 +19,14 @@
 
     fn add_tags(&mut self, tags: &[Tag], private: bool) -> Result<(), Error> {
         for tag in tags.iter() {
-            let bookmark = match tag.parse()? {
+            let parsed = match tag.parse() {
+                Ok(parsed) => parsed,
+                Err(e) => {
+                    tracing::warn!("Skipping unparseable bookmark tag {:?}: {}", tag, e);
+                    continue;
+                }
+            };
+            let bookmark = match parsed {
                 ParsedTag::Event {
                     id,
                     recommended_relay_url,
